#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402427 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=\\pm 1$ then the level curves are $xy=\\pm 1,$ which are hyperbolas.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curves are $xy=0,$ which consists of the axes $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\nSo, only statements “$c=1$ gives a hyperbola” and “$c=0$ gives the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402609 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} & 3 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} & 3 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} & 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-\\frac{4}{7}+\\frac{5}{7})-3(-\\frac{1}{49}+\\frac{1}{49})-\\frac{1}{7}(\\frac{5}{7}-\\frac{4}{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}-3\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{49}-\\frac{1}{49} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$N$ is a singular matrix” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403272 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=({t}^{3}-1){e}^{t},\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=({t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-1){e}^{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{({t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-1){e}^{t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{({0}^{3}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}-1){e}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{(-1)\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403279 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403307 :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403314 :question/problem "![](images/86/86dcfa13b7863c08b348855e776a6df709ecac499af02c2479cfdf484eeaf328.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403321 :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d90adc7e7badb9c43763febb960b1a8a2b13884c4ce913110264d5c1998ac7b.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $12$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(12,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=12.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403335 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(2+3)-2(1+6)-1(-1+4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 7-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =20-14-3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(T)=3\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$T$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403468 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $z=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x \\\\ z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =(-4x-1)\\text{ }i-4y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3) & =(-4\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1)\\,i-4(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =-3\\,i-4\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ and passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}⟨-3,-4,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403475 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z,\n$$ find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}+0-\\frac{1}{z{x}^{2}}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}+0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =0+0-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}x}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle ∇f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k.\n$$ \n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $\\mathbf{i}$, $\\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf{k}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=-1.$ Substituting these into the third equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}(-1)}{{z}^{2}(-1)} & =0 \\\\ 1-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{(-1)^{2}yz} & =0 \\\\ z+y & =0 \\\\ y & =-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $z=1,$ we have $y=-1$ and when $z=-1,$ we have $y=1.$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}$ at two points: $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403571 :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-4xy=-5{x}^{2}+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy & =-5{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2(y-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(10x-4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{i}$, we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{i}=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{i} & =0 \\\\ (2(y-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(10x-4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i} & =0 \\\\ 2(y-2x) & =0 \\\\ y & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-4x(2x)+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403675 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-t,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-4,\\,t\\ge -1,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $1-t\\le 2$ for all $t\\ge -1.$ Consequently, $x=1-t\\le 2.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that ${t}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $t.$ Consequently, $y={t}^{2}-4\\ge -4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x\\le 2$ and $y\\ge -4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403822 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,y=2{t}^{3}-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}=-2, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}-6t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-1}{4{t}^{3}-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{2}-1}{4(1)^{3}-6(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\,-\\frac{5}{2}\\,)}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\frac{2}{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403981 :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac2bba143473bc68e3a578c008a5de3cc36727d984bfba5d5e0da95462c08f77.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403988 :question/problem "![](images/5f/5fb18775e625816f119cf95c09051eba41fced187f4e4d1e7964921f4b877288.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,2),$ $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403995 :question/problem "![](images/85/85f4441b721fa0c059c6783357c01b40de46314cf82ad501f9addc518226169f.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-4,-4),$ $a=4$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404002 :question/problem "![](images/07/07ec13485a828c8f5cd99ae2fbc3f7444135ba3c1cdf790762edb01b9726317e.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,2),$ $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404009 :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d45616d089e8f5d078ff48c949b26d0920074ab4b611ec71b491a0e18fd293c.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,1)$ with horizontal radius $1$ and vertical radius $2,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=1$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405036 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green's theorem and changing to polar coordinates $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with the integrand simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c2/c2718471780b5ea4f4b0298624d14a22efb317ef4e8302b9269eeeec9449ca0d.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 6,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405043 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5},\\,x+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green's theorem and changing to polar coordinates $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with the integrand simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(x+2y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405050 :question/problem "![](images/65/6571fcdf94818485c2a39e90e018d46e9c95e1acbe0a8cf2f67c688d160a347c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405132 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$ at the point $(0,0,2),$ with parameter $t=0$ at this point." :question/worked-solution "First, note that we can write the equation of our surface as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we set $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1,\n$$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,2) & =2(0)\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{(2)}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the vector form of the equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,0,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{∇}f(0,0,2)=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406696 :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=2x{y}^{3}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{3}z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x{y}^{3}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int {z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y{z}^{2}+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz+{h}^{′}(z) & ={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406697 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}y+3{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{3}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406698 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=(2x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2y+x+z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(y-2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+x+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y-2z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+xy+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+x+z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2y+x+z \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2y+z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (2y+z)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+yz+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}+yz+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y-2z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2z & =y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2z \\\\ h(z) & =-{z}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz-{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406699 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{sin}y+y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(x\\operatorname{cos}y+x)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+{g}^{′}(y) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ g(y) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406700 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the functoin $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{3}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406771 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+vw \\\\ {v}^{2} \\\\ w+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+vw,\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{2},\\,z(u,v,w)=w+v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & w & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2v & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(2v-0) \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2v & =0 \\\\ v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=0$ defines the plane $v=0,$ i.e., $uw$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406772 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+vw,\\,y=2-uv,\\,z={v}^{2}-w.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & w & v \\\\ -v & -u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-u & 0 \\\\ 2v & -1\\end{vmatrix}+(-v)(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}w & v \\\\ 2v & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u(u-0)+v(-w-2{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{3}-vw.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406786 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-2,-3,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3,1) & =(4(-2)-(-3))\\,i-(-2)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406787 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2y-x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right).$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right)-2y+x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right)-2y+x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right)-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right)-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2y}+1)i+(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{2{y}^{2}}\\right)-2)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{y}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(\\frac{1}{2}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{1}-2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x-2) & =\\frac{3}{2}(y-1) \\\\ -6(x-2) & =3(y-1) \\\\ -2(x-2) & =y-1 \\\\ -2x+4 & =y-1 \\\\ 2x+y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406798 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =1(x-1) \\\\ z & =x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ z &=x-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x-1$ (or $x-z+-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407316 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,0,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407566 :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=6x+2y?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get $6x+2y=-1,$ which gives the line $y=-3x-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $6x+2y=0,$ which gives the line $y=-3x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get $6x+2y=2,$ which gives the line $y=-3x+1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/d6/d6c45af410df1ae974062b777b856b3960e72ad13e14d37a48e5a92cebf74cd1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as both $x$ and  $y$ increase. \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa601a2bb31bb8c009f554361a79135daf1d7abd2779eaf1ae8b2431a01eafa.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407631 :question/problem "![](images/58/584016c88e5916984e181118d52caab95dba1fd0d861e0fd248a4e023d1b07da.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{div}\\,F<0$ is true, whereas statements $\\text{div}\\,F=0$ and $\\text{div}\\,F>0$ are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors decrease as we move horizontally and vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is solenoidal” is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{div}\\,F<0$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407632 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\operatorname{cos}\\pi x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\operatorname{sin}\\pi y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{2z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \nEvaluate $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(0,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}\\pi y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2z}) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\\pi x+\\operatorname{sin}\\pi y+\\pi y\\operatorname{cos}\\pi y+2{e}^{2z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(0,1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F})(0,1,0) & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(0)+\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi )+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi )+2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0+0-\\pi +2 \\\\  & =2-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407846 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\frac{{0}^{3}-2(0)}{{2}^{3}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{y({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{y}({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}\\cancel{y}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-1$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2}$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-{x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2(-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $\\frac{1}{3}$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407848 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}-2}{{2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2}-2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2})^{2}-4}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy-2}{(xy-2)(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} & =\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1+2)(\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{16}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407849 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\frac{1+(-1)}{\\sqrt{2-1}-\\sqrt{2+(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z})(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(x-y)-(x+z)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{-(y+z)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(y+z)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{\\cancel{(y+z)}} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z}) & =-(\\sqrt{2-1}+\\sqrt{2+(-1)}) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"}]}
